WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.318
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.927 --> 00:00:07.760
SPEAKER: Welcome to the
Quick Start Tutorial

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.710
for Managed Kubernetes.

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:14.020
Today, we're going to deploy
a containerized application.

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:16.360
First, we'll need to enable
the Kubernetes Engine

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.230
API on our project.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:22.500
In the navigation menu,
click on API and Services,

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.070
and then click Enable
APIs and Services.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.348
Here, search for
Kubernetes Engine

00:00:28.348 --> 00:00:29.640
and select it from the results.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:36.980
On the Kubernetes
Engine API page,

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:41.050
click Enable to enable
it on the project.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:43.687
Next, let's activate the
Cloud Shell in our console

00:00:43.687 --> 00:00:44.895
where we'll run our commands.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.970
Once we're here, we can
specify the default zone

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:55.630
that we want the G Cloud
and kubectl commands to use.

00:00:55.630 --> 00:01:01.600
Type gcloud config set compute
zone followed by a zone.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.864
For this demonstration,
let's use us-east1.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.785
With our default zone set, let's
create a Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.060
A cluster consists of at
least one cluster master

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:19.490
and multiple worker
machines called nodes.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:21.790
These nodes are
Compute Engine VMs that

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:25.230
run the Kubernetes processes.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:30.210
An application is deployed to a
cluster and runs on its nodes.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:34.020
Enter the command gcloud
container clusters create

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.360
and a cluster name.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.160
Once our cluster
is created, we'll

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:43.310
need authentication credentials
to interact with it.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:46.370
We'll get that with
gcloud container clusters

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:51.040
get credentials followed
by the cluster name.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.802
The get credentials subcommand
will configure Q cuddle,

00:01:53.802 --> 00:01:55.760
the command line interface
for running commands

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.840
against Kubernetes clusters to
use a cluster we just created

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.360
in any subsequent commands.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.580
Now, it's time to deploy a
containerized application

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:07.660
to our cluster.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:12.300
Type kubectl, create deployment,
and a name for deployment.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.000
Let's go with hello server.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.840
We've provided a sample web
application called Hello App.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.510
We can specify its Google
Container Registry URL

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:26.430
with the dash dash image tag.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:28.278
Our application is
deployed, but if we

00:02:28.278 --> 00:02:29.820
want to see what it
looks like, we'll

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.230
need to expose it
to the internet

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.330
by creating a
Kubernetes service.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:36.780
Type kubectl exposed deployment
followed by our deployment

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.160
name, hello server.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.620
We'll specify a type,
load balancer, a port, 80,

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:49.720
and a target port, 8080.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.090
This means that the load
balancer will initialize

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:55.090
the public port 80 to the
internet and route traffic

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:58.390
to port 8080 of the application.

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:01.540
After a few minutes, the load
balancer should be all set up

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:05.750
and have an assigned
external IP address.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:09.650
Kubectl get service followed
by our deployment name

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.480
will show us the
IP address we'll

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:14.230
use to access our application.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:17.620
Enter that IP address
in a new tab or window,

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:18.760
and there you have it.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:20.890
We've just deployed a
containerized web application

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:21.880
to Kubernetes.

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:25.530
[MUSIC PLAYING]

